   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the village law and the public officers law, in relation to\nqualifications to hold certain positions in the village of Scarsdale",
    "basePrintNo": "S9309",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9309-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3-300, Vil L; amd §3, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Village Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9309\n\nSPONSOR: STEWART-COUSINS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the village law and the public officers law, in relation\nto qualifications to hold certain positions in the village of Scarsdale\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow for certain positions in the Village of Scarsdale to be filled\nby individuals residing outside of the Village.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Section 3-300 of the village law is amended by adding a new\nsubdivision 32 to provide that notwithstanding any provisions of this\nchapter or any other chapter, general law, local law, ordinance, or\nspecial act, the village board of trustees of the village of Scarsdale,\nWestchester county, may, by resolution, provide that the office of court\nclerk, building inspector, assistant building inspector, village clerk,\nassistant village clerk, village treasurer, assistant village treasurer,\nassessor, village engineer, or village planner of such village may be\nheld by a person who is not a resident of such village, provided that\nsuch person resides within Westchester county or an adjoining county\nwithin the state of New York, except that the village treasurer may also\nreside within Dutchess county.\n\nSection 2. Section 3 of the public officers law is amended by adding a\nnew subdivision 72 to provide that in the village of Scarsdale, West-\nchester county, the provisions of this section requiring a person to be\na resident of the political subdivision or municipal corporation of the\nstate for which he or she shall be chosen or within which his or her\nofficial functions are required to be exercised, shall not prevent a\nperson from holding the office of court clerk, building inspector,\nassistant building inspector, village clerk, assistant village clerk,\nvillage treasurer, assistant village treasurer, assessor, village engi-\nneer, or village planner, provided that such person resides within West-\nchester county or an adjoining county within the state of New York,\nexcept that the village treasurer may also reside within Dutchess coun-\nty. Provided, however that persons performing the functions of such\nappointed positions in any other municipality shall be a resident of\nsuch municipality unless otherwise provided by an act of the state\nlegislature.\n\nSection 3 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Village of Scarsdale has recently undergone some turnover in\nappointed positions. The Village's experiences in trying to locate qual-\nified personnel to fill those vacancies have taken a toll in terms of\ntime, finances and overall staff morale. The requirement that appointed\npositions be filled with residents of Scarsdale is incongruous in\ntoday's housing market. Housing continues to be more unaffordable rela-\ntive to income. Aside from this economic disparity, we are seeing more\nand more instances post-pandemic where viable candidates simply do not\nwish to work on a fulltime basis and or work remotely. All of this\nlimits the ability of the Village to attract the most qualified candi-\ndates, particularly here in Westchester County where competition is\nstrained, and geographic scope is largely limited to begin with. By\nallowing the Scarsdale Court Clerk, Building Inspector, Assistant Build-\ning Inspector, Village Clerk, Assistant Village Clerk, Village Treasur-\ner, Assistant Village Treasurer, Assessor, Village Engineer, and Village\nPlanner, to reside outside the Village of Scarsdale, but within West-\nchester County, an adjacent county to Westchester in New York, and in\nDutchess County, there is more of an opportunity to recruit and retain\nqualified candidates.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
